---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Gloss from '../components/Gloss.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import DataTable from '../components/DataTable.astro'
import { assertion, series } from '../lib/feeds'
import { bar, line } from '../lib/chart-spec'

const farmland = assertion('the-county-is-two-thirds-farmland')

const rented = assertion('most-of-the-farmland-is-rented')

const yields = assertion('an-acre-yields-five-times-what-it-did')

const fourYears = assertion('a-fifth-of-the-farmland-went-in-four-years')

const sawtooth = assertion('the-farmland-did-not-drain')

// The complete record: eighteen census years of land in farms, with the farm count beside it and
// the annual rate of change across each interval. Typed out because the corpus holds it as one
// measure and this table is what the phase was for.
const fullFarmland = [
  ['1910', '240,472', '2,939', '—'],
  ['1920', '241,488', '2,909', '+102'],
  ['1925', '228,249', '2,722', '−2,648'],
  ['1930', '230,424', '2,684', '+435'],
  ['1935', '235,791', '3,016', '+1,073'],
  ['1940', '225,630', '2,574', '−2,032'],
  ['1945', '227,038', '2,511', '+282'],
  ['1950', '227,944', '2,560', '+181'],
  ['1954', '215,018', '2,097', '−3,232'],
  ['1959', '206,047', '1,755', '−1,794'],
  ['1964', '211,196', '1,659', '+1,030'],
  ['1969', '215,862', '1,611', '+933'],
  ['1974', '205,706', '1,354', '−2,031'],
  ['1978', '195,604', '1,244', '−2,526'],
  ['1982', '197,256', '1,202', '+413'],
  ['1987', '201,190', '1,148', '+787'],
  ['2002', '188,150', '968', '−869'],
  ['2022', '178,921', '897', '−461'],
]

const fourYearsSpec = bar(
  fourYears.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  fourYears.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Acres of farmland lost, by stretch', labelValues: true },
)

// The seven census years the corpus now holds for land in farms. Typed out because they come
// from four different sources and the point of the table is that they are one series.
const farmSeries = [
  ['1910', '2,939', '240,472', '92.5%', 'Thirteenth Census'],
  ['1950', '2,560', '227,944', '—', '1954 volume, prior-year column'],
  ['1954', '2,097', '215,018', '81.9%', '1954 Census of Agriculture'],
  ['1982', '1,202', '197,256', '—', '1987 volume, prior-year column'],
  ['1987', '1,148', '201,190', '77.7%', '1987 Census of Agriculture'],
  ['2002', '968', '188,150', '—', 'Census of Agriculture'],
  ['2022', '897', '178,921', '69.4%', 'Census of Agriculture'],
]

// Bars, not a line. The 1910 observation and the 2002 one are ninety-two years apart and
// nothing measured between them; a line would draw that segment, and its shape is exactly
// what this corpus refuses to assert.
const sawtoothSpec = line(sawtooth.figures.map((f) => f.label), [
    { name: 'Acres in farms', y: sawtooth.figures.map((f) => f.value) },
  ], {
  yTitle: 'Acres',
})

const farmlandSpec = bar(
  farmland.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  farmland.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Acres in farms' },
)

const houses = assertion('the-houses-went-where-the-city-is-not')

const housesSpec = bar(
  houses.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  houses.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Single-family houses authorized, 1990–2025', labelValues: true },
)

const permitDecades = [
  ['1990–1999', 2252, 2941, '225.2'],
  ['2000–2009', 1952, 2159, '195.2'],
  ['2010–2019', 587, 1025, '58.7'],
  ['2020–2025', 638, 844, '106.3'],
]

// Typed out: eleven streams the gazetteer files in another county because that is where they end.
const lost = [
  ['Auglaize River', 'Defiance'],
  ['Big Run', 'Putnam'],
  ['Cranberry Creek', 'Putnam'],
  ['Cranberry Run', 'Putnam'],
  ['Flat Fork', 'Putnam'],
  ['Jennings Creek', 'Putnam'],
  ['Little Riley Creek', 'Hardin'],
  ['Ottawa River', 'Putnam'],
  ['Plum Creek', 'Putnam'],
  ['Rattlesnake Creek', 'Putnam'],
  ['Sugar Creek', 'Putnam'],
]

const yieldSpec = bar(
  yields.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  yields.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Bushels per acre', labelValues: true },
)

const farms = assertion('the-farms-did-not-go-anywhere')

// Two bars that are nearly the same height. That is the finding, and a line between two points
// would draw a trend across a gap that holds no observations.
const farmSpec = bar(farms.figures.map((f) => f.label), farms.figures.map((f) => f.value), {
  yTitle: 'Acres of land in farms, Allen County',
  labelValues: true,
})

const address = assertion('an-address-is-not-a-municipality')
---

<Base
  title="Ground"
  lede="Two thirds of Allen County is farms. This page is about what stands on that ground and what happens to the lines drawn on it — who owns it, what it grows, and where the farmland went."
>
  <div class="reading">
  <section>
    <h2>The other nine-tenths</h2>
    <p>
      Two thirds of Allen County's surface is farmland. The federal agricultural census has been
      taken every five years since long before any of the rest of this, and the volume for 1909
      found the county 92.5 per cent farms.
    </p>
    <Gloss>
      Eleven phases went to the third of the county that lives in Lima before one of them measured
      a farm.
    </Gloss>
    <AssertionCard assertion={farmland} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="farmland"
      title="Land in farms"
      caption="Census of Agriculture. There is no observation between 1910 and 2002: the bars are five years apart at the right-hand end and ninety-two years apart at the left, and nothing here says what the line did in between."
      columns={['Census', 'Acres in farms']}
      rows={farmland.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="farmland" set:html={JSON.stringify(farmlandSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      That is the shape of the gap and it is worth stating plainly. Of the 61,551 acres that left
      the county's farms, 52,322 went before the first census this corpus can read. It knows the
      farmland went. It cannot say whether it went in one decade or in nine, and a county that lost
      fifty thousand acres to drainage, to the oil field, to Lima's annexations or to the interstate
      lost them for four different reasons.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>More than half of it is worked by somebody who does not own it</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={rented} />
    <p>
      The county had 2,939 farms in 1910 and 897 in 2022, and this site does not subtract the one
      from the other. A farm in 1910 was land under one manager, with nothing under three acres
      counted unless it produced $250; a farm in 2022 is any place selling $1,000 or more. Two
      counts on two thresholds are not a series. What is comparable is acres, animals and bushels,
      and those are what every figure on this page is made of.
    </p>

    <h3>An acre yields five times what it did</h3>
    <AssertionCard assertion={yields} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="yields"
      title="Yield per harvested acre, 1909 and 2022"
      caption="Production divided by harvested acres, from the two censuses' own figures for both terms. Neither census reports yield this way; the ratio is computed here because both print the numerator and the denominator."
      columns={['Crop and year', 'Bushels per acre']}
      rows={yields.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="yields" set:html={JSON.stringify(yieldSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Soybeans and corn are 84.9 per cent of the county's cropland. The rest is 9,252 acres of
      wheat, 2,686 of hay and haylage, 793 of popcorn on fourteen operations, and $1.66 million of
      vegetables, fruit, nursery stock and everything else together. The crops that fed the horses
      went with them: 30,156 acres of oats in 1909 and 11 acres in 2022, and where there were 3,107
      acres of rye there is now one operation in the county growing it, whose acreage the census
      withholds because one operation is all there is.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The oil did not take the farms</h2>
    <p>
      The oil did not take the farms. The same 1910 volume that counts Lima's factory workers
      counts them: 2,939 in 1910 against 2,858 in 1900, covering 240,472 of the county's 259,840
      acres. More farms than before the boom, on two per cent less land, with more of that land
      improved.
    </p>
    <Gloss>
      Allen County was described here as a farming county reoriented by oil into an industrial one
      for nine phases before a single farm was measured.
    </Gloss>
    <p>
      What the oil years did to farming here shows up in one figure only, and it is a price. An acre
      of Allen County farmland went from $38.39 in 1900 to $76.28 in 1910, and the county's farm
      property rose 89.0 per cent against 58.7 per cent for Ohio as a whole. Whether that was oil
      money bidding up the land is the obvious reading, and the corpus has nothing that would
      separate it from the general rise in Midwestern land values over the same decade.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={farms} />

    <ChartFigure
      id="farms"
      title="Land in farms, Allen County, 1900 and 1910"
      caption="Thirteenth Census of Agriculture. The county was already more than half city by population in 1910 — Lima held 30,508 of 56,580 — while still being nine-tenths farm by ground. This is the only agricultural census the corpus has read, so nothing here shows when the farmland began to go."
      columns={['Census year', 'Acres in farms']}
      rows={farms.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
      height={280}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="farms" set:html={JSON.stringify(farmSpec)} is:inline />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The houses went where the city is not</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={houses} />
    <p>
      Between 1990 and 2025 Allen County authorized 5,437 new single-family houses. Lima — a third
      of the county's population — was issued 367 of them, 6.8 per cent. American Township took
      1,098, Shawnee Township 981 and Bath Township 643: the three townships that wrap around the
      city account for half of everything the county built in thirty-six years.
    </p>
    <ChartFigure
      id="new-houses"
      title="Single-family houses authorized, 1990–2025, the four busiest places"
      caption="A permit is authorization to build, not a building. It says nothing about whether the work was done or whether anything was demolished to make room."
      columns={['Place', 'Houses']}
      rows={houses.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="new-houses" set:html={JSON.stringify(housesSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      The money follows the same line. A new house in Shawnee Township was reported to the permit
      office at $211,559 and one in Lima at $95,879 — the city is the cheapest of the county's ten
      busiest places to build in, by a factor of two against its southern neighbour. Lima is also
      the only place in the county that builds apartments: 734 of the county's 774 dwellings in
      buildings of five units or more were authorized inside it, and Delphos has the other forty.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      title="Thirty-six years, four decades"
      caption="Reported construction value across the whole span is $968,246,660, in the dollars of each year and not adjusted. Twenty-three of the thirty-six years contain no apartment building of five units or more at all."
      columns={['Decade', 'Single-family', 'All dwellings', 'Houses a year']}
      rows={permitDecades}
    />
    <p>
      The peak year is 2004, at 331 houses, and the floor is 2011, at 35 — an 89.4 per cent fall in
      seven years, and the recovery has not come close to reversing it. But the sharper number is
      the one that pairs with the census. Between 1990 and 2020 the county lost 7,549 people and
      Lima lost 9,970, which means the ground outside the city gained 2,421. Allen County's
      depopulation is, over these thirty years, half relocation — and the permits say which half
      mile it moved to.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>It did not drain. It sawtoothed.</h2>
    <p>
      Everything above rests on seven census years. There are eighteen. The whole printed run of
      the Census of Agriculture is scanned on the National Agricultural Statistics Service's own
      host, table by table, at the address the old Cornell agricultural archive redirects to. Ten
      Ohio volumes close the gap, and each of them prints the census before it, so ten of the
      eighteen years are printed twice in two different books and agree to the acre.
    </p>
    <Gloss>
      That address had been recorded here as redirecting, without the scans behind it.
    </Gloss>
    <AssertionCard assertion={sawtooth} open />
    <ChartFigure
      id="farmland-full"
      title="Acres in farms, Allen County, eight of eighteen census years"
      caption="Acres rather than share of the county: the land-area denominator moves between volumes — 259,840 acres in 1925, 262,405 in 1969 — and a line of percentages would carry that as if it were farmland."
      columns={['Year', 'Acres in farms']}
      rows={sawtooth.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
      height={320}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="farmland-full" set:html={JSON.stringify(sawtoothSpec)} is:inline />
    <DataTable
      title="Land in farms, every census year from 1910"
      caption="The last column is the average annual change since the row above. Farm counts are not comparable across the definition changes of 1959, 1974 and 1978 — the 1959 change alone removed 75 Allen County farms from the count without anyone selling anything."
      columns={['Year', 'Acres in farms', 'Farms', 'Acres a year']}
      rows={fullFarmland}
    />
    <p>
      The forty years that looked like the flattest stretch in the record are the least flat. They
      net 313 acres a year and contain 23,400 acres of loss and 10,872 of recovery — a rise of
      1,016, a fall of 13,239, a recovery of 7,542, a fall of 10,161 and a recovery of 2,314, in
      that order. An average taken across two endpoints described none of it.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      One year in eighteen disagrees with itself. The 1964 volume gives Allen County 211,196 acres
      in farms and the 1969 volume gives 211,195 for the same census — one acre, one bureau, five
      years apart, with nothing on either page to catch it. It is the second publisher in this record to print its own figure two ways, after the Ohio Secretary of State's two readings of the 1896 vote, and the rule is the same: the census's own year wins and the
      divergence is published.
    </p>

    <h3>A fifth of the farmland went in four years</h3>
    <AssertionCard assertion={fourYears} />
    <p>
      The fall was not steady. The printed census volumes for 1954 and 1987 are scanned on
      archive.org, and each of them prints the census before it in the next column — so two books
      give four census years across the ninety-two between the ends of the record.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      title="Land in farms, seven census years"
      caption="The 1950 and 1982 figures are the prior-year columns of the 1954 and 1987 volumes, which is what makes each pair comparable: one publisher, one table, one set of column headings. The proportions are the ones the volumes print, against land areas that themselves move a little between censuses."
      columns={['Year', 'Farms', 'Acres in farms', 'Share of the county', 'Source']}
      rows={farmSeries}
    />
    <ChartFigure
      id="farmland-stretches"
      title="Acres of farmland lost, by stretch of the record"
      caption="Four bars of roughly equal height. Three of them are forty, twenty-eight and forty years long. The second is four."
      columns={['Stretch', 'Acres lost']}
      rows={fourYears.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="farmland-stretches" set:html={JSON.stringify(fourYearsSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Between 1950 and 1954 the county lost 12,926 acres of farmland — 3,232 a year, against 313 a
      year for the forty years before it — and 18.1 per cent of its farms. It is not a local event:
      Ohio lost 4.66 per cent of its land in farms over the same four years and this county 5.67,
      and Ohio lost 11.2 per cent of its farms against Allen's 18.1. The county falls harder than
      the state and in the same direction, which is what a national turn looks like. The known break
      in the census's definition of a farm is 1959, outside this pair.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two other things the record now dates. Between 1954 and 1982 the county lost 17,762 acres of
      farmland and gained 16,749 acres of harvested cropland — the two nearly cancel, so what left
      was pasture, woodland and farmstead rather than the ground under the corn. And the rise of
      3,934 acres between 1982 and 1987 happens while an acre of Allen County farmland loses a
      quarter of its value, $2,023 to $1,504. In the same five years the county's "other cropland"
      went from 3,593 acres to 20,121. Land retired from production is still land in farms.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the crop changeover has a date. In 1949 the county harvested 26,213 acres of oats and
      17,393 of soybeans; in 1954, 22,498 of oats and 22,991 of soybeans. The crop that did not
      exist in this county in 1909 had passed the one that fed its horses before the interstate was
      built — and the oats survived that crossing at 22,498 acres and were down to eleven by 2022.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why the ground is drawn and not described</h2>
    <p class="lede-narrow">
      A table of edges can say a site is in a place. It cannot show you that the site is two
      miles outside the boundary of the place named. This is the corpus's clearest case of
      exactly that, and it was wrong from the first commit until two authorities were asked.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={address} open />
    <p class="caution">
      The corollary is the one the corpus states about itself: nearness is not containment. A
      dot inside a shape on this map is a dot inside a shape. Where a node's location claim is
      an <strong>inference</strong>, its badge says so, and no amount of visual coincidence
      upgrades it.
    </p>
  </section>

    <section class="onward">
      <h2>Where this goes on</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/where-the-lines-moved">Where the lines moved</a>
          <span class="why">Fifty-seven dated annexations, and a county that lost land it never lost.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/the-weather-the-farms-are-in">The weather the farms are in</a>
          <span class="why">The winters warmed and the summers did not; three more weeks between the frosts.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/what-crosses-the-water">What crosses the water</a>
          <span class="why">Three hundred and sixty-four bridges, and a state that replaced its own while the county kept its.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/the-river-measured">The river, measured</a>
          <span class="why">The city drinks from a river nobody gauges.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/when-the-villages-began">When the villages began</a>
          <span class="why">The county's villages have incorporation dates now.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/a-hog-county">A hog county</a>
          <span class="why">The hogs multiplied while the farms keeping them halved.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read/counted-two-ways">Counted two ways</a>
          <span class="why">Two authorities disagree about how many named things stand here, and both are right.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/read#ground">The reading room</a>
          <span class="why">Every shorter piece filed under this section.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>

  <script>
    import { renderCharts } from '../scripts/charts'
    renderCharts()
  </script>
</Base>
